\section{Oil palm residue}{

Oil palm residue is replaced rather than taken from the pin (whep#1424).
Read as gross residue, the pin's oil palm residue is far above an oil palm
plantation's whole above-ground net primary production, fruit included:
81\% of 17.3 Mg C, so about 30 t DM at the 0.47 kg C per kg DM of
\link{biomass_coefs}, per hectare and year in the industrial plantation of
Wakhid, Hirano, Dariah & Agus (2022), \emph{Mires and Peat} 28:02,
\doi{10.19189/map.2021.snpg.sta.2288}. In Malaysia it is several times the
country's whole solid oil palm biomass, mill residues included.

The anchor is Malaysia's field residue: fronds (46 Mt) and trunks (14 Mt)
of dry matter on 4.85 Mha planted in 2010, Exhibit 1 and p. 10 of Agensi
Inovasi Malaysia (2013), \emph{National Biomass Strategy 2020}, version 2.0
(FAOLEX mal228571). Mill residues (empty fruit bunches, fibre, shells) are
part of the fruit bunch and are not field residue, so they are left out.
The anchor is a gross residue, so it replaces the pin's figure after the
recovery rate has been undone and the ratio re-keyed; neither touches it.
The anchored dry matter is converted to fresh mass with the oil palm
\code{Residue_kgDM_kgFM} of \link{biomass_coefs}, so \code{value_dm} is the anchor
exactly. That coefficient is 0.396, the dry matter of fresh oil palm fronds
(39.6\% as fed, n = 3) in Heuzé, Tran, Sauvant & Lebas (2015), \emph{Oil palm
fronds and oil palm crop residues}, Feedipedia,
\url{https://www.feedipedia.org/node/6916} (whep#1516). Fronds are 46 of the
60 Mt; trunks, whose sap is 75-80\% of their weight, are wetter, so the
fresh mass is if anything understated. It sets the fresh mass booked to
\code{Firewood}, not the dry matter.
}

\section{Sugar cane residue}{

Sugar cane trash is replaced in the same way (whep#1551). IPCC (2006) Table
11.2 and the 2019 Refinement's Table 11.2 give sugar cane no line, and the
pin's ratio put world trash at 0.206 t of dry matter per tonne of cane in
both 2010 and 2020, above every field measurement found:\tabular{lllr}{
   source \tab where \tab trash \tab t DM per t cane \cr
   Hassuani, da Silva & Neves (2005), p. 394 \tab Brazil \tab tops, dry and green leaves \tab 0.14 \cr
   Thompson (1966), p. 333 \tab Natal, dryland \tab dead leaves and immature tops \tab 1/6 = 0.167 \cr
   Donaldson et al. (2008), p. 533, Table 3 \tab South Africa, irrigated \tab dead and green leaves, tops excluded \tab 1/9.3 = 0.108 \cr
}


The default is Hassuani et al.'s 140 kg of dry matter per tonne of cane
stalk. Its global application is \strong{provisional}: these independent
regional measurements do not establish a constant for every country and
year, and Donaldson excludes tops. The Brazilian value is the average
of Copersucar's field experiments across variety, age
and soil, which lies between the two South African figures. Brazil grows
43\% of the pin's world cane in 2010 and 41\% in 2020. The sources are:
Hassuani SJ, da Silva JEAR, Neves JLM (2005), \emph{Sugarcane trash recovery
alternatives for power generation}, Proc. ISSCT 25: 394-402; Thompson GD
(1966), \emph{The production of trash and its effects as a mulch on the soil
and on sugarcane nutrition}, Proc. S. Afr. Sug. Technol. Ass. 40: 333-342;
and Donaldson RA, Redshaw KA, Rhodes R, van Antwerpen R (2008), \emph{Season
effects on productivity of some commercial South African sugarcane
cultivars, II: trash production}, Proc. S. Afr. Sug. Technol. Ass. 81:
528-538.

The anchored dry matter is converted to fresh mass with the sugar cane
\code{Residue_kgDM_kgFM} of \link{biomass_coefs} (0.48), so \code{value_dm} is the
anchor exactly. World cane trash falls from 345 to 235 Tg of dry matter in
2010 (all residue 5403 to 5293 Tg, -2.0\%) and from 384 to 261 Tg in 2020
(6463 to 6341 Tg, -1.9\%). Choose \code{"pin"} to keep the pin's ratio.
}

\section{The batch pin on the build path}{

Two further aliases were published in that same 2025-07-14 batch ---
\code{crop_residues} and \code{bilateral_trade}, alongside the four above, between
12:33:43Z and 12:33:50Z. Unlike the four, these two are read on the default
build path, which is why no warning is attached to them: every commodity
balance build reads them, so a warning at the read is noise rather than
information. \code{bilateral_trade} has since been regenerated by this package
(#1122), so \code{crop_residues} is the only one still registered at its
2025-07-14 version.

They did \strong{not} share a provenance despite sharing a timestamp. Each was
established separately (#1054), because the timestamp alone establishes
nothing.
\subsection{\code{crop_residues} is predecessor output}{

Read by \code{\link[=get_primary_residues]{get_primary_residues()}}, and from there by
\code{\link[=build_commodity_balances]{build_commodity_balances()}}. All 475,688 of its \code{Product} rows equal the
\code{primary_prod} pin's \code{tonnes} values exactly --- no key unmatched on either
side, no value differing at a relative tolerance of 1e-6 --- so it is a
downstream artifact of the same predecessor run, carrying that run's
production series into the commodity balance.

Its residue quantities are those production numbers times a
residue-to-product ratio that varies by year, and times a \strong{recovery
rate}: the pin holds the residue the predecessor recovered from the field,
not the residue produced (#1195). The predecessor's code
(\code{Global/R/crop_npp.r}, then \code{afsetools::residue_use()}) writes each row as

\code{product * kg_residue_kg_product_FM * HI_changes(region, year) * residue_dm_product_dm(region, category) / residue_dm_product_dm("West Europe", category) * recovery_rates(region, category)},

with region and category keyed as in \code{residue_recovery.csv}. Rebuilt from
the predecessor's own coefficient tables, that formula reproduces all
425,934 pin rows whose region it can key, at a relative tolerance of 1e-6. The
year variation is \code{HI_changes} alone: a harvest-index change factor per
region, given at eight anchor years from 1910 to 2000, linear in between
and flat after 2000 (East Europe 1.10 in 1962 to 1.00 in 2000), which the
predecessor attributes to Krausmann et al. (2013), \emph{PNAS} 110:10324, Table
M2 (assumed, unverified). It is a regional historical adjustment, not a
yield response, and after 2000 the ratio of every region and category is
constant. That is why \code{biomass_coefs$kg_residue_kg_product_FM} alone
reproduces only 3,189 of the 472,790 comparable keys. Of the 33,411 keys
that carry a residue of exactly 0, 30,620 are cells whose recovery rate is
0 (fodder crops in seven of the eight regions; roots and tubers, cassava,
sugar beet and dry beans in West Europe and in North America and Oceania)
and 1,919 are crops whose \code{biomass_coefs} ratio is 0.

WHEP ships the recovery rate and the regional ratio the predecessor used
(both equal in all 160 cells), but not the \code{HI_changes} table. So
\code{\link[=get_primary_residues]{get_primary_residues()}} undoes the recovery exactly, dividing by the same
rate, but cannot recompute the predecessor's residue from production. It
then re-keys the regional ratio on Wirsenius's own region membership, and
estimates cereal residue (#1448), and that of the non-cereal crops with
their own IPCC Table 11.2 line (#1518), from the pin's production and
harvested area instead of reading it.

It is not a small input. The pin holds 7.63 Gt of recovered residue for
2010 (Straw 3.60 Gt, Other crop residues 2.49 Gt, Firewood 1.54 Gt) and
327.7 Gt over 1961--2021, which \code{\link[=get_primary_residues]{get_primary_residues()}} turns into 7.64
Gt (Straw 3.92 Gt, Other crop residues 2.82 Gt, Firewood 0.90 Gt) and 366
Gt of residue produced. 3,998 of its 249,095 output rows carry \code{NA} polity
columns because the pin is name-keyed.

Driving the same residue model off a fresh \code{\link[=get_primary_production]{get_primary_production()}} would
move those numbers. Measured for 2010 at (\code{area_code}, \code{item_prod}): 1.958
Gt of current production sits on 6,425 keys the pin never sees (809 Mt of
it primary crops, including the modelled temporary-grassland item), 64.8 Mt
on 296 keys is pin-only, and of the 8,010 shared keys 538 disagree, putting
1.102 Gt --- 11.01\% of the pin's shared-key mass --- more than 1\% apart.
The largest class is rice, 673.9 Mt in the pin against 463.3 Mt fresh, a
ratio of 0.6876: current code puts rice on a milled-equivalent basis while
the pin's is paddy, which is a basis difference rather than an error, since
straw scales with the field crop. The second is fodder, where the current
build is close to twice the pin on every forage and silage item, the pin
predating that work. Replacing the pin therefore means choosing a residue
model, which is a science decision and not a refresh. For cereals that
model is chosen by \code{get_primary_residues(cereal_residue = )}, and for the
non-cereals with an IPCC line by \verb{non_cereal_residue =}; both still run on
the pin's production.
}

